.docs/

# Private working notes. `docs/` is public by default and the publish gate
# scans everything tracked in it (`git ls-files`), so anything carrying a
# home path, a real namespace, a credential location or a client's
# infrastructure goes here instead.
#
# ⚠ Never `git add -f` into this directory. Force-adding into an ignored
# path is exactly how five design notes drifted 49-119 lines behind their
# working copies with git reporting nothing: once a file is both tracked
# and ignored, `git status` stops mentioning it.
docs/private/

.venv/
*.pyc
__pycache__/
*.egg-info/
dist/
build/
.pytest_cache/
.ruff_cache/
.env
.DS_Store

# This checkout is rite's own source. RUNTIME state under `.rite/` here is
# residue from manually running a command (`rite watchdog`,
# `rite scheduler-tick`, ...) against this repo, and is ignored.
#
# Authored config is not residue, and three files are:
#
#   - `.rite/gitleaksignore` — the publish gate reads its suppressions from
#     here (gate.suppression.DEFAULT_SUPPRESSION_PATH), so ignoring it
#     wholesale means this repo can never suppress a false positive and the
#     publish-gate workflow can never go green.
#   - `.rite/config.yaml` — this project's own rite config, and since
#     §10.2 it also records the credential NAMES this project uses (never
#     values). That file is what makes an auto-generated credential scope
#     survive a fresh clone: the secret is in the keychain, the name is
#     here, and a clone that cannot read the name cannot find the
#     credential. `.rite/*` swallowed it — measured with `git check-ignore
#     -v .rite/config.yaml`, which named this file's `.rite/*` line — so
#     the negation below is load-bearing, not tidiness.
#   - `.rite/review-checklist.md` — the checklist `rite review` merges and
#     every reviewer of this repo is supposed to work. Ignoring it is why
#     `rite review` printed "No checklist items found." in the project that
#     ships the checklist, and why rite's own review rounds were worked
#     against `templates/review-checklist.md` instead: nobody here had ever
#     experienced the thing a user gets.
#
# This mirrors `scaffold.AUTHORED_CONFIG`, which is the same split for a
# project rite manages: ignore the directory's contents, re-include the
# short, stable set of shared files. Note `.rite/*` rather than `.rite/` —
# git does not descend into an excluded directory, so the negations below
# only work if the directory itself is not excluded.
.rite/*
!.rite/config.yaml
!.rite/gitleaksignore
!.rite/review-checklist.md
